<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burundian Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1993">1993</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Burundian Civil War was fought from 1993 to 2005 between the Tutsi-dominated army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> rebel groups. It began after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melchior-ndadaye">Melchior Ndadaye</a>, Burundi's first democratically elected Hutu president, in October 1993, and an estimated 300,000 people died. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha">Arusha</a> Agreement of 2000 and later ceasefires led to a new constitution and elections in 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burundian-civil-war.jpg" alt="Burundian Civil War" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RTNB (Burundi National Radio and Television) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:People_fleeing_during_1993_Burundian_genocide.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>German and then Belgian colonial rulers governed Burundi through its Tutsi monarchy, keeping the Tutsi minority above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> majority. After independence in 1962, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a>'s coup of 1966 began 25 years of Tutsi military regimes. In 1972 Hutu militants attacked Tutsi with the declared aim of annihilating them, and the army answered with mass reprisals against Hutu; the dead together are estimated at over 100,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-buyoya">Pierre Buyoya</a>, who seized power in 1987, attempted reforms, but Hutu revolts in the north and the army's response left between 5,000 and 50,000 dead, and Hutu rebel groups such as PALIPEHUTU-FNL and FROLINA took up arms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the free elections of June and July 1993 the Front for Democracy in Burundi (FRODEBU) defeated Buyoya's UPRONA. On 21 October Tutsi extremist officers murdered President Ndadaye, and 50,000 to 100,000 people, about as many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> as Tutsi, died within a year as militias and gangs on both sides killed. The coup regime collapsed, but in 1994 the minister Léonard Nyangoma led a FRODEBU faction into rebellion as the CNDD-FDD. Ndadaye's successor Cyprien Ntaryamira died in the plane crash of 6 April 1994 with the Rwandan president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juvenal-habyarimana">Juvénal Habyarimana</a>, and the state lost control to the army and to ethnic militias.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> and Tanzania let <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> rebels keep bases on their soil, while from 1995 Rwanda's government aided Burundi's and its troops attacked refugee camps that sheltered rebels. In 1996 Buyoya took power again in a coup. He brought the Tutsi militias under control and sought talks, but neighbouring states imposed an embargo and the rebels grew stronger, killing about 300 Tutsi in one attack on 20 July 1996.</p><p>The army raised armed and mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> militias, the Guardians of the Peace, which numbered 3,000 fighters by late 1997, and both sides used child soldiers widely. Talks opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha">Arusha</a> on 15 June 1998 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> and, after his death in 1999, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A transitional government took office in October 2001, with Domitien Ndayizeye succeeding Buyoya as president on 30 April 2003. Two wings of the CNDD-FDD joined the agreement in December 2002, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> mission arrived. The FNL fought on, killing 160 Congolese Tutsi refugees at Gatumba in August 2004. A referendum approved the constitution in 2005, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-nkurunziza">Pierre Nkurunziza</a> of the FDD was sworn in as president in August that year. The FNL later signed a ceasefire, though its dissidents fought on into 2008. A Truth and Reconciliation Commission was set up in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burundian Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
